The pathway begins with glutamate binding to GRM1, leading to GNAQ activation and subsequent PLCB activity. This results in IP3 production, which activates ITPR1, releasing Ca²⁺. The Ca²⁺ then enters the mitochondria via MCU, leading to MPTP, which increases mitochondrial DNA damage. This process is linked to CYCS, a mitochondrial enzyme involved in the electron transport chain.

ATXN3 is part of the Ataxin family, which is associated with spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA). Specifically, ATXN3 is involved in Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 3 (SCA3). Mutations in ATXN3 cause the accumulation of toxic ataxin protein, leading to neurodegeneration, particularly in the cerebellum, resulting in ataxia, motor dysfunction, and other neurological symptoms. This allele contributes to the progression of SCA3.

